Title: A CHRISTMAS CAROL
Credit: Adapted by
Author: Charles Dickens
Source: Adapted from A Christmas Carol by Charles Dickens
Draft date: 7/31/2026

FADE IN:

INT. SCROOGE AND MARLEY COUNTING-HOUSE - DAY

Cold, bleak, biting weather outside. Fog pours through every chink and keyhole. Inside a gloomy office, EBENEZER SCROOGE, a sharp-featured old man with frost on his wiry eyebrows and a pointed nose, sits at a high desk in his heavy dark coat. Through an open door, BOB CRATCHIT, a thin clerk with a threadbare coat and a long white comforter, shivers over a tiny fire of a single coal.

(SOUND: Wind howling outside, footsteps stamping on frozen pavement stones)

FRED, a ruddy-faced, handsome young man with sparkling eyes and a warm woolen cloak, bursts through the door in a breath of fog.

FRED
A merry Christmas, uncle! God save you!

SCROOGE
Bah! Humbug!

FRED
Christmas a humbug, uncle! You don't mean that, I am sure?

SCROOGE
I do. What right have you to be merry? What reason have you to be merry? You're poor enough.

FRED
Come, then. What right have you to be dismal? What reason have you to be morose? You're rich enough.

SCROOGE
Bah! Humbug!

FRED
Don't be cross, uncle!

SCROOGE
What else can I be, when I live in such a world of fools as this? If I could work my will, every idiot who goes about with "Merry Christmas" on his lips, should be boiled with his own pudding, and buried with a stake of holly through his heart. He should!

FRED
Uncle! Keep Christmas in your own way, and let me keep it in mine.

SCROOGE
Keep it! But you don't keep it. Let me leave it alone, then. Much good may it do you! Much good it has ever done you!

FRED
I have always thought of Christmas time as a good time; a kind, forgiving, charitable, pleasant time. And therefore, uncle, though it has never put a scrap of gold or silver in my pocket, I believe that it has done me good, and will do me good; and I say, God bless it!

Bob Cratchit quietly applauds from his tank, then hastily pokes his tiny fire, extinguishing the last spark.

(SOUND: Iron poker scraping against cold iron grate)

SCROOGE
Let me hear another sound from you, and you'll keep your Christmas by losing your situation!

Scrooge turns his glare back to Fred.

FRED
Don't be angry, uncle. Come! Dine with us to-morrow.

SCROOGE
Good afternoon!

FRED
I want nothing from you; I ask nothing of you; why cannot we be friends?

SCROOGE
Good afternoon!

FRED
I am sorry, with all my heart, to find you so resolute. So A Merry Christmas, uncle!

SCROOGE
Good afternoon!

Fred exits into the fog, offering a warm greeting to Bob as he leaves.

Immediately, SOLICITOR, a portly gentleman in a dark tailcoat holding a clipboard and quill, enters the doorway.

SOLICITOR
Scrooge and Marley's, I believe? At this festive season of the year, Mr. Scrooge, it is desirable that we should make some slight provision for the Poor and destitute.

SCROOGE
Are there no prisons?

SOLICITOR
Plenty of prisons.

SCROOGE
And the Union workhouses? Are they still in operation?

SOLICITOR
They are. Still, I wish I could say they were not.

SCROOGE
The Treadmill and the Poor Law are in full vigour, then?

SOLICITOR
Both very busy, sir. We are endeavouring to raise a fund to buy the Poor some meat and drink. What shall I put you down for?

SCROOGE
Nothing!

SOLICITOR
You wish to be anonymous?

SCROOGE
I wish to be left alone. I don't make merry myself at Christmas and I can't afford to make idle people merry. Those who are badly off must go there.

SOLICITOR
Many can't go there; and many would rather die.

SCROOGE
If they would rather die, they had better do it, and decrease the surplus population. Good afternoon, gentleman!

The Solicitor bows stiffly and exits into the gloom.

(SOUND: Heavy timber door slamming shut)

Scrooge checks his pocket watch and steps down from his stool. Bob Cratchit steps out of his small cell, pulling his long comforter tight.

SCROOGE
You'll want all day to-morrow, I suppose?

BOB CRATCHIT
If quite convenient, sir.

SCROOGE
It's not convenient, and it's not fair. A poor excuse for picking a man's pocket every twenty-fifth of December! But I suppose you must have the whole day. Be here all the earlier next morning.

BOB CRATCHIT
I will indeed, sir.

EXT. SCROOGE'S COURTYARD AND DOORSTEP - NIGHT

Thick fog and black frost cling to the cobblestones of a dark, narrow yard enclosed by gloomy stone buildings. Scrooge, holding a key, approaches his heavy wooden front door.

(SOUND: Wind whistling through the narrow stone archway, key rattling in iron lock)

Scrooge reaches for the massive iron knocker. Suddenly, the knocker glows with a dim, uncanny luminescence, transforming into MARLEY'S FACE—livid, with ghostly spectacles shoved up on its forehead and hair stirred by unseen air.

Scrooge freezes, his eyes wide in terror.

(SOUND: Low spectral groan humming through the timber)

Scrooge blinks heavily. In an instant, it is a plain iron knocker once again.

SCROOGE
Pooh, pooh!

Scrooge turns the key sturdily and steps inside.

INT. SCROOGE'S BEDCHAMBER - NIGHT

A gloomy room with a small, dying fire on a Dutch-tiled hearth. Scrooge, now in his nightcap, flannel dressing-gown, and slippers, sits in an armchair stirring a small saucepan of gruel over the faint embers.

(SOUND: A disused brass bell hanging on the wall begins to swing, chiming softly at first, then ringing loudly)

Every bell in the house joins in a sudden chorus. Then, total silence.

(SOUND: Heavy iron chains clanking across wine casks deep in the cellar below)

Scrooge grips his chair arms, pale and trembling.

(SOUND: Heavy footsteps dragging chains ascending stairs, approaching the chamber door)

SCROOGE
It's humbug still! I won't believe it.

The heavy wooden door remains closed, but JACOB MARLEY'S GHOST passes straight through the solid wood. The phantom is transparent, dressed in waistcoat and boots, dragging a long, heavy chain made of cash-boxes, keys, padlocks, ledgers, and steel purses wrapped around his waist.

(MUSIC: Eerie, low-frequency pitch rising subtly)

(SOUND: Clanking metal links striking the floorboards)

SCROOGE
How now! What do you want with me?

MARLEY
Much!

SCROOGE
Who were you?

MARLEY
In life I was your partner, Jacob Marley.

SCROOGE
You don't believe in me.

MARLEY
I don't. You may be an undigested bit of beef, a blot of mustard, a crumb of cheese. There's more of gravy than of grave about you, whatever you are!

Marley's Ghost raises a frightful cry and shakes his heavy chain with appalling force. He unbinds the bandage around his head, and his lower jaw drops down upon his breast.

(SOUND: Terrifying ghostly shriek accompanied by violent metal clattering)

Scrooge falls to his knees, clasping his hands.

SCROOGE
Mercy! Dreadful apparition, why do you trouble me?

MARLEY
It is required of every man that the spirit within him should walk abroad among his fellowmen. If that spirit goes not forth in life, it is condemned to do so after death. I wear the chain I forged in life! Is its pattern strange to you?

Scrooge quakes, glancing at the empty floor around his knees.

SCROOGE
Speak comfort to me, Jacob!

MARLEY
I have none to give. I am here to-night to warn you, that you have yet a chance and hope of escaping my fate. You will be haunted by Three Spirits.

SCROOGE
Is that the chance and hope you mentioned, Jacob? I think I'd rather not.

MARLEY
Without their visits, you cannot hope to shun the path I tread. Expect the first to-morrow, when the bell tolls One.

Marley steps backward toward the window, which glides open on its own.

(SOUND: Distant wailing voices echoing outside in the freezing air)

Marley floats out into the dark night, dissolving into the foggy air among countless moaning phantoms bound in chains. Scrooge rushes to the window, slams it shut, and stumbles back to bed in sheer exhaustion.

INT. SCROOGE'S BEDCHAMBER - NIGHT (LATER)

The room is jet black. Scrooge lies in his curtained bed, peering into the gloom.

(SOUND: Church clock bell chiming four quarters, then striking ONE deep, resonant toll)

A sudden flash of light illuminates the room. The bed curtains are pulled aside by an unseen force.

Scrooge sits up, staring in awe.

Standing beside the bed is the GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST—a strange figure like a child yet like an old man, with unwrinkled skin, long white hair, a tunic of pure white trimmed with summer flowers, and a bright jet of light springing from the crown of its head. Under its arm, it carries a brass extinguisher cap.

(SOUND: Soft hum of light vibrating in the quiet chamber)

SCROOGE
Are you the Spirit, sir, whose coming was foretold to me?

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
I am!

SCROOGE
Who, and what are you?

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
I am the Ghost of Christmas Past. Your past.

SCROOGE
What business brings you here?

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
Your welfare! Your reclamation, then. Take heed!

The Ghost extends a strong, gentle hand and clasps Scrooge's arm.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
Rise! and walk with me!

INT. SCHOOLROOM - DAY (PAST)

Bright, crisp winter sunlight streams through dusty high windows into a vast, bare, cold room filled with empty wooden desks.

(SOUND: Distant wind rustling through bare trees, faint crackle of a small fireplace)

At a desk near a meager hearth sits YOUNG SCROOGE—a lonely boy in solitary study, reading a book with sad, quiet focus.

Old Scrooge stands beside the Ghost of Christmas Past, watching his younger self with tearful eyes.

SCROOGE
Poor boy! Poor forgotten boy as I used to be!

The schoolroom door bursts open. LITTLE FAN, a bright-eyed little girl in a woolen bonnet, darts across the floor, throws her arms around Young Scrooge's neck, and kisses him.

LITTLE FAN
I have come to bring you home, dear brother! Home, home, home!

YOUNG SCROOGE
Home, little Fan?

LITTLE FAN
Yes! Home, for good and all. Father is so much kinder than he used to be! You're to be a man, and are never to come back here!

She laughs merrily and drags him by the hand toward the door.

(SOUND: Laughter of children fading into street sounds)

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
Always a delicate creature, whom a breath might have withered. But she had a large heart! She died a woman, and had, as I think, children.

SCROOGE
One child. My nephew.

INT. FEZZIWIG'S WAREHOUSE - NIGHT (PAST)

Warm, golden lamplight floods a spacious, swept warehouse decorated with holly.

(MUSIC: Lively, cheerful fiddle music playing "Sir Roger de Coverley")

OLD FEZZIWIG, a jovial old gentleman in a Welsh wig and high waistcoat, stands atop a desk clapping his hands with boundless joy.

FEZZIWIG
Yo ho, my boys! No more work to-night. Christmas Eve, Dick. Christmas, Ebenezer! Let's have the shutters up before a man can say Jack Robinson!

YOUNG ADULT SCROOGE—now a cheerful young apprentice with dark hair—and his fellow apprentice DICK WILKINS rush into the street, slamming wooden shutters into place with rapid energy.

Old Scrooge watches with an ecstatic smile, tapping his foot to the lively tune. On the floor, Fezziwig and MRS. FEZZIWIG lead twenty couples in a booming, joyous country dance.

(SOUND: Laughter, clapping hands, stomping feet in rhythm)

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
A small matter to make these silly folks so full of gratitude. He has spent but a few pounds of your mortal money: three or four perhaps. Is that so much that he deserves this praise?

SCROOGE
It isn't that, Spirit. He has the power to render us happy or unhappy; to make our service light or burdensome. The happiness he gives is quite as great as if it cost a fortune.

Scrooge stops, feeling the Ghost's piercing gaze upon him.

SCROOGE
I... I should like to be able to say a word or two to my clerk just now. That's all.

EXT. PARK BENCH - DAY (PAST)

A bleak winter garden with dry fallen leaves drifting across the path.

(SOUND: Cold wind blowing through bare branches)

Young Adult Scrooge, now older with subtle lines of care and greed tightening his face, sits beside BELLE, a fair young woman in a dark mourning dress. Tears sparkle in her eyes.

BELLE
It matters little to you, very little. Another idol has displaced me; and if it can cheer and comfort you in time to come, I have no cause to grieve.

YOUNG ADULT SCROOGE
What Idol has displaced you?

BELLE
A golden one. You are changed. When our contract was made, you were another man.

YOUNG ADULT SCROOGE
I was a boy.

BELLE
Your own feeling tells you that you were not what you are. That which promised happiness when we were one in heart, is fraught with misery now that we are two. I release you. With a full heart, for the love of him you once were.

Belle stands up, wipes a tear, and walks away into the misty path. Young Adult Scrooge sits in sullen silence.

Old Scrooge covers his face in anguish.

SCROOGE
Spirit! Show me no more! Conduct me home. Why do you delight to torture me?

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
These are shadows of the things that have been. That they are what they are, do not blame me!

INT. SCROOGE'S BEDCHAMBER - NIGHT

Old Scrooge struggles wildly with the Ghost of Christmas Past right beside his bed.

(SOUND: Whooshing wind and intense burning flare of light)

Scrooge seizes the heavy brass extinguisher cap and presses it down firmly over the Ghost's head. The Ghost shrinks beneath it, but an unbroken flood of brilliant light streams out from beneath the rim onto the floor.

Exhausted, Scrooge collapses onto his bed and falls into a deep sleep.

INT. SCROOGE'S LIVING ROOM - NIGHT

Scrooge awakens to a roaring fire. He gets up, opens the door, and steps into his adjoining sitting room.

The room is brilliantly transformed—hung with green holly, mistletoe, and ivy. Heaped on the floor in a grand throne are turkeys, geese, roasts, sausages, mince-pies, plum-puddings, and glowing bowls of punch.

(SOUND: Roaring hearth fire, rich bubbling simmer of warm punch)

(MUSIC: Warm, majestic brass chords blooming)

Seated on the feast is the GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT—a majestic, smiling giant in a fur-trimmed green robe, bare chest, dark curls under a holly wreath, holding a glowing torch shaped like Plenty's horn.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
Come in! Come in, and know me better, man!

Scrooge steps forward submissively, bowing his head.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
I am the Ghost of Christmas Present. Look upon me! You have never seen the like of me before!

SCROOGE
Never. Spirit, conduct me where you will. To-night, if you have aught to teach me, let me profit by it.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
Touch my robe!

Scrooge grips the green mantle firmly.

INT. CRATCHIT HOUSE - DAY

The simple, cramped four-room home of Bob Cratchit. Scrooge and the Ghost of Christmas Present stand invisible in the corner.

MRS. CRATCHIT, in a twice-turned dress decorated with cheap ribbons, and her daughter BELINDA lay the cloth. PETER CRATCHIT stirs potatoes over the fire.

(SOUND: Potatoes bubbling loudly in saucepan, joyful chatter of children)

The door opens. BOB CRATCHIT enters carrying TINY TIM upon his shoulder. Tim is a frail boy holding a small wooden crutch, his legs bound in iron frames.

MRS. CRATCHIT
And how did little Tim behave?

BOB CRATCHIT
As good as gold, and better. He told me, coming home, that he hoped the people saw him in the church, because he was a cripple, and it might be pleasant to them to remember upon Christmas Day, who made lame beggars walk, and blind men see.

Bob sets Tim down on his stool near the hearth.

(SOUND: Silverware clattering, cheers as a steaming roast goose is placed on the table)

The family sits around the table. Bob pours hot spiced drink from a jug into two tumblers and a custard cup.

BOB CRATCHIT
A Merry Christmas to us all, my dears. God bless us!

TINY TIM
God bless us every one!

Scrooge gazes at the frail boy with deep emotion.

SCROOGE
Spirit, tell me if Tiny Tim will live.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
I see a vacant seat in the poor chimney-corner, and a crutch without an owner, carefully preserved. If these shadows remain unaltered by the Future, the child will die.

SCROOGE
No, no! Oh, no, kind Spirit! Say he will be spared.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
If these shadows remain unaltered by the Future, none other of my race will find him here. What then? If he be like to die, he had better do it, and decrease the surplus population.

Scrooge hangs his head in deep shame.

BOB CRATCHIT
Mr. Scrooge! I'll give you Mr. Scrooge, the Founder of the Feast!

MRS. CRATCHIT
The Founder of the Feast indeed! I wish I had him here. I'd give him a piece of my mind to feast upon! I'll drink his health for your sake and the Day's, Robert, not for his. Long life to him!

The family drinks the toast quietly, a momentary chill falling over the table.

INT. FRED'S HOUSE - NIGHT

A bright, cheerful drawing room warmly lit by lamps.

(SOUND: Boisterous laughter, piano music playing a festive tune)

Fred, his wife, and several party guests are mid-game, roaring with laughter. Scrooge and the Ghost of Christmas Present watch from beside the fireplace.

FRED
I thought of an animal—a live, disagreeable, savage animal that growls and grunts, lives in London, and walks the streets!

PLUMP SISTER
I know what it is, Fred! It's your Uncle Scro-o-o-o-oge!

FRED
It is indeed! He has given us plenty of merriment, and it would be ungrateful not to drink his health. Uncle Scrooge!

GUESTS
Uncle Scrooge!

Fred raises his glass amidst cheering. Scrooge smiles broadly, joining in the unbidden phantom cheer.

EXT. GRAVEYARD - NIGHT

A cold, bleak, foggy open ground outside the city. The chimes ring three-quarters past eleven.

(SOUND: Church bell ringing 11:45 p.m. with slow, heavy tolls)

The Ghost of Christmas Present's hair has grown snowy white and aged. Scrooge notices two wretched, gaunt, wolfish children clinging to the Spirit's robes.

SCROOGE
Spirit! Are they yours?

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
They are Man's. This boy is Ignorance. This girl is Want. Beware them both, but most of all beware this boy!

SCROOGE
Have they no refuge or resource?

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
Are there no prisons? Are there no workhouses?

(SOUND: Heavy clock tower strikes TWELVE loud, booming strokes)

The Ghost of Christmas Present vanishes into thin air on the twelfth stroke.

From the thick mist, a dark, draped phantom glides silently along the ground. The GHOST OF CHRISTMAS YET TO COME approaches, shrouded completely in a black garment, revealing nothing but one pale, outstretched hand pointing forward.

(SOUND: Low, ominous wind moaning across dry weeds)

(MUSIC: Deep, ominous low drone sound)

Scrooge drops to his knees before it.

SCROOGE
I am in the presence of the Ghost of Christmas Yet To Come? Ghost of the Future! I fear you more than any spectre I have seen. Lead on, Spirit!

INT. OLD JOE'S RAG-SHOP - NIGHT

A filthy, foul-smelling shop crammed with heaps of rusty keys, iron chains, and dirty rags.

(SOUND: Squeaking rusty door hinges, crackle of a crude charcoal stove)

OLD JOE, a grey-haired rascal smoking a pipe, sits by the stove.

MRS. DILBER, the laundress, and CHARWOMAN slink in carrying heavy bundles. Charwoman throws her bundle down.

CHARWOMAN
Open the bundle, old Joe! Who's the worse for the loss of a few things like these? Not a dead man, I suppose! He frightened every one away from him when he was alive, to profit us when he was dead!

Old Joe unrolls dark velvet curtains and a fine woolen shirt.

OLD JOE
Bed-curtains! You don't mean to say you took 'em down, rings and all, with him lying there?

CHARWOMAN
Yes I do! Why not?

(SOUND: Clinking coins as Old Joe counts out money from a flannel bag)

Scrooge stands beside the silent, pointing Ghost, shuddering in horror.

SCROOGE
Spirit! I see, I see. The case of this unhappy man might be my own. My life tends that way, now.

INT. CRATCHIT HOUSE - DAY

The same small living room, now terribly quiet.

(SOUND: Distant muffled church chime, faint weeping)

Mrs. Cratchit and her daughters sew in silence. Peter holds a closed Bible.

Bob Cratchit walks slowly through the door, his comforter wrapped tight. His children gather around him silently.

BOB CRATCHIT
I went to see the place today. It would have done you good to see how green a place it is. I promised him that I would walk there on a Sunday. My little, little child! My little child!

Bob breaks down in tears, burying his face in his hands.

Scrooge watches in heartbreaking remorse.

SCROOGE
Let me see some tenderness connected with a death, Spirit, or that dark room will be for ever present to me.

EXT. CHURCHYARD - NIGHT

A gloomy, overgrown churchyard hemmed in by tall houses. Weeds choke the neglected stone graves.

(SOUND: Harsh winter gale howling across headstones)

(MUSIC: Tense, dramatic pitch peaking)

The Ghost of Christmas Yet To Come stands motionless, its dark shroud silhouetted against the dark sky, pointing down with its single pale finger toward a forgotten grave.

Scrooge creeps forward, trembling violently.

SCROOGE
Before I draw nearer to that stone to which you point, answer me one question. Are these the shadows of the things that Will be, or are they shadows of things that May be, only?

The finger remains fixed upon the stone. Scrooge kneels and brushes away the frost, reading the carved inscription: EBENEZER SCROOGE.

SCROOGE
Am I that man who lay upon the bed? No, Spirit! Oh no, no! Hear me! I am not the man I was. Assure me that I yet may change these shadows you have shown me, by an altered life!

Scrooge desperately clutches the Spirit's dark sleeve.

SCROOGE
I will honour Christmas in my heart, and try to keep it all the year. The Spirits of all Three shall strive within me! Oh, tell me I may sponge away the writing on this stone!

The Spirit's hand trembles. The dark hood and garment shrink, collapse, and transform into a carved wooden bedpost.

INT. SCROOGE'S BEDCHAMBER - MORNING

Bright, golden sunlight fills the room.

(SOUND: Church bells clanging, hammering, and ringing out joyous peals in full chorus)

Scrooge is on his knees, hugging his bedpost, crying and laughing wildly.

SCROOGE
I will live in the Past, the Present, and the Future! The Spirits of all Three shall strive within me. Oh Jacob Marley! Heaven, and the Christmas Time be praised for this!

Scrooge scrambles out of bed, wildly trying to put on his trousers upside down. He rushes to the window and throws it open to the crisp, clear air.

SCROOGE
No fog, no mist! Clear, bright, glorious sunlight!

Scrooge spots BOY in Sunday clothes walking past in the street below.

SCROOGE
What's to-day, my fine fellow?

BOY
To-day! Why, CHRISTMAS DAY.

SCROOGE
It's Christmas Day! The Spirits have done it all in one night! Do you know whether they've sold the prize Turkey that was hanging up in the Poulterer's? The big one?

BOY
What, the one as big as me? It's hanging there now!

SCROOGE
Go and buy it, and tell 'em to bring it here! Come back with the driver in less than five minutes and I'll give you half-a-crown!

BOY
Walk-ER!

The Boy darts off like a shot. Scrooge chuckles, rubbing his hands together.

SCROOGE
I'll send it to Bob Cratchit's! He sha'n't know who sends it. It's twice the size of Tiny Tim!

EXT. CITY STREET - DAY

Sunlight glints off snow-dusted roofs. Joyful citizens stroll the street.

(SOUND: Joyful bustle of street chatter, church chimes)

Scrooge, immaculately dressed in his finest coat, struts down the street with a beaming smile. He stops the Solicitor who visited his office yesterday.

SCROOGE
My dear sir! How do you do? A merry Christmas to you, sir!

SOLICITOR
Mr. Scrooge?

Scrooge leans in and whispers in his ear. The Solicitor's jaw drops in utter shock.

SOLICITOR
Lord bless me! My dear Mr. Scrooge, are you serious?

SCROOGE
Not a farthing less! A great many back-payments are included in it, I assure you. Will you do me that favour?

SOLICITOR
My dear sir, I don't know what to say to such munificence!

SCROOGE
Don't say anything, please. Come and see me!

INT. FRED'S HOUSE - DAY

A richly set dining table brimming with holiday roast and festive glasses.

(SOUND: Warm dining banter, clinking wine glasses)

Fred and his wife look up in surprise as the door opens. Scrooge sidles his head in with a beaming, humble smile.

SCROOGE
Fred!

FRED
Why, bless my soul! Who's that?

SCROOGE
It's I. Your uncle Scrooge. I have come to dinner. Will you let me in, Fred?

Fred leaps up and shakes Scrooge's arm with enormous warmth.

FRED
Let you in! Wonderful! Welcome, Uncle Scrooge!

Fred's wife smiles warmly as Scrooge sits down at the festive table, radiant with joy.

INT. SCROOGE AND MARLEY COUNTING-HOUSE - MORNING

The clock on the wall strikes NINE-FIFTEEN.

(SOUND: Clock ticking loudly, bell striking 9:15)

Scrooge sits at his desk with his door wide open.

BOB CRATCHIT scurries in, rushing to his stool in the tank, pulling off his hat and comforter in a flurry, scribbling madly with his quill.

Scrooge steps out, adopting his old growl.

SCROOGE
Hallo! What do you mean by coming here at this time of day?

BOB CRATCHIT
I am very sorry, sir. I am behind my time. It's only once a year, sir. It shall not be repeated.

SCROOGE
Now, I'll tell you what, my friend, I am not going to stand this sort of thing any longer! And therefore...

Scrooge leaps forward and claps Bob heartily on the back, beaming with laughter.

SCROOGE
And therefore I am about to raise your salary!

Bob freezes in disbelief, holding his ruler like a defensive weapon.

SCROOGE
A merry Christmas, Bob! A merrier Christmas, Bob, my good fellow, than I have given you for many a year! I'll raise your salary, and assist your struggling family, and we will discuss your affairs this afternoon over a bowl of smoking bishop, Bob! Make up the fires, and buy another coal-scuttle before you dot another i, Bob Cratchit!

Bob laughs with tears of relief and joy streaming down his cheeks.

(MUSIC: Warm, glorious orchestral finale soaring)

Scrooge looks out the window at the snow.

(SOUND: Faint laughter of Tiny Tim echoing happily)

TINY TIM (V.O.)
God bless us, Every One!

FADE OUT.

THE END
